The os module in Python provides tools to interact with the operating system, including file management, environment variables, and process control.
Here’s a detailed breakdown of what the os module offers and how you can use it effectively:

What Is the os Module?
The os module is part of Python’s standard library and allows you to perform operating system-dependent tasks in a portable way. This includes working with directories, files, system paths, and environment variables.

Commonly Used Functions
Here are some of the most useful functions provided by the os module:
📁 Directory and File Operations
•	os.getcwd() – Returns the current working directory.
•	os.chdir(path) – Changes the current working directory to the specified path.
•	os.listdir(path) – Lists all files and directories in the given path.
•	os.mkdir(path) – Creates a new directory.
•	os.makedirs(path) – Creates intermediate directories as needed.
•	os.remove(path) – Deletes a file.
•	os.rmdir(path) – Removes a directory.
•	os.rename(src, dst) – Renames a file or directory.

🧭 Path Utilities
•	os.path.join() – Joins one or more path components.
•	os.path.exists(path) – Checks if a path exists.
•	os.path.isfile(path) – Checks if a path is a file.
•	os.path.isdir(path) – Checks if a path is a directory.

🌍 Environment Variables
•	os.environ – A dictionary representing the user’s environment variables.
•	os.getenv(key) – Gets the value of an environment variable.
•	os.putenv(key, value) – Sets the value of an environment variable.

⚙️ Process Management
•	os.system(command) – Executes a shell command.
•	os._exit(status) – Exits the process immediately.
•	os.abort() – Terminates the process with a core dump.

Example Usage
import os

# Print current working directory
print("Current Directory:", os.getcwd())

# List contents of current directory
print("Directory Contents:", os.listdir())

# Create a new folder
os.mkdir("new_folder")

# Rename the folder
os.rename("new_folder", "renamed_folder")

# Remove the folder
os.rmdir("renamed_folder")







